{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789455039,"nonce":"s833wwqWvl8nsRbQMZIfdS11z38=","title":"Collector: a collection that frees nothing should earn a larger budget","message":"The budget after a collection is max(live, floor), so a program building a table collects at 16, 32, 63, 125 and 250 MB, marking a live set that never has anything dead in it. On bench_records five collections mark 0 blocks freed each time and cost 9 + 17 + 34 + 79 + 154 = 293 ms of a 672 ms run; on bench_pipeline 136 of 321 ms. Marking runs at about 1.6 GB/s, so the work is proportional to the sum of the live sizes at each trigger, twice the final live set.\n\nLever: grow the budget by survival. A collection that frees under a small share of what it reached (say a tenth) doubles the factor the next budget is made from (budget = factor x live, factor capped, say at 8); one that frees a good share resets the factor to 1. On records that is collections at 16, 48 and 192 MB and roughly a third of the marking; on a program with real garbage nothing changes. Peak memory rises only in a build-up phase, where the program is about to hold that much anyway.\n\nSecond order: the mark loop itself (consider: range check, slab hash lookup, bit test per word) is where the rest goes; sticky mark bits would need a write barrier the compiled code does not have.","files":null}]}